exports[`composeReport (KTD7) > snapshots the real incident fixtures > agent-promptpwnd 1`] = `
"Blastgate: reachable attacker-controlled path to a secret in acme/widgets reaches GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write) at .github/workflows/issue-triage.yml:14 (+1 more)

## Blastgate automated security report

This is an automated report from Blastgate, a scanner for attacker-to-secret paths in CI workflows. It is limited to proven paths: Blastgate only reports a fail when it can trace a reachable path from attacker-controllable input to a secret or credential in \`acme/widgets\`.

2 reachable paths found. Each lists where the input enters, what it reaches, why, and how to break the path. How the scanner decides what is reportable: https://github.com/jwolberg/blastgate/blob/main/docs/threat-model.md

**How to respond:** if this is accurate, breaking any edge on a path (the fix lines below) closes it. If it is wrong, or you accept the risk, reply here or close this report to decline; no further report will be filed for these findings.

**Disclosure:** we suggest a 90-day coordinated-disclosure window from the date of this report. Blastgate does not publish this finding; any advisory is yours to publish.

---

### ❌ untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body) → … → 🔑 GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write)

\`untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body)\` → \`.github/workflows/issue-triage.yml#triage\` → \`GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write)\`

- **why:** untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body) — an outsider can steer the agent google-github-actions/run-gemini-cli＠v0.1.21 in job .github/workflows/issue-triage.yml#triage into reading credential GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write) and getting it out. Rule of Two — direct trigger: held (any issues author triggers it (the action has no actor check)); sensitive access: held (its tools can read GEMINI_API_KEY; a contents:write GITHUB_TOKEN); exfiltration: held (it has a shell or network tool).
- **fix:** Break a leg: restrict who can trigger google-github-actions/run-gemini-cli＠v0.1.21 (keep its write-access gate, add an author_association guard), deny it shell/network tools it does not need, and keep GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write) out of the agent job (the Agents Rule of Two).
- **sink:** credential \`GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write)\`
- **at:** \`.github/workflows/issue-triage.yml:14\` → \`GITHUB_TOKEN (contents:write, issues:write)\`
- **OWASP:** \`ASI01:2026\`, \`MCP10:2025\`

---

### ❌ untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body) → … → 🔑 GEMINI_API_KEY

\`untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body)\` → \`.github/workflows/issue-triage.yml#triage\` → \`GEMINI_API_KEY\`

- **why:** untrusted issues text reaches job triage (github.event.issue.title, github.event.issue.body) — an outsider can steer the agent google-github-actions/run-gemini-cli＠v0.1.21 in job .github/workflows/issue-triage.yml#triage into reading credential GEMINI_API_KEY and getting it out. Rule of Two — direct trigger: held (any issues author triggers it (the action has no actor check)); sensitive access: held (its tools can read GEMINI_API_KEY; a contents:write GITHUB_TOKEN); exfiltration: held (it has a shell or network tool).
- **fix:** Break a leg: restrict who can trigger google-github-actions/run-gemini-cli＠v0.1.21 (keep its write-access gate, add an author_association guard), deny it shell/network tools it does not need, and keep GEMINI_API_KEY out of the agent job (the Agents Rule of Two).
- **sink:** credential \`GEMINI_API_KEY\`
- **at:** \`.github/workflows/issue-triage.yml:14\` → \`GEMINI_API_KEY\`
- **OWASP:** \`ASI01:2026\`, \`MCP10:2025\`

---

_Scanned by Blastgate 0.1.0 at commit \`aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab\`._"
